      "text": "To determine the mean of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\), we need to first find the value of \\(n\\). The median of a set of numbers is the middle number when the set is ordered. Given that the median is 9, we need to consider two cases: when the set has an odd number of elements (which it does, with 5 elements) and when the set has an even number of elements (which it does, with 5 elements).\n\n**Case 1: The set has an odd number of elements.**\nIf the set is ordered, it would look like this: \\(\\{n, n+5, n+6, n+9, n+15\\}\\). The median is the third number in the ordered set, so we have:\n\\[n + 6 = 9.\\]\nSolving for \\(n\\), we get:\n\\[n = 3.\\]\n\n**Case 2: The set has an even number of elements.**\nIf the set is ordered, it would look like this: \\(\\{n, n+5, n+6, n+9, n+15\\}\\) or \\(\\{n, n+5, n+6, n+15, n+9\\}\\). In either case, the median is the average of the third and fourth numbers. Since the median is 9, we have:\n\\[\\frac{(n+6) + (n+9)}{2} = 9.\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\\frac{2n + 15}{2} = 9.\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 2, we get:\n\\[2n + 15 = 18.\\]\nSubtracting 15 from both sides, we get:\n\\[2n = 3.\\]\nDividing both sides by 2, we get:\n\\[n = \\frac{3}{2}.\\]\nSince \\(n\\) must be an integer for the set to be valid (as the elements are given to be distinct integers), this case is not possible.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solution is \\(n = 3\\). The set is \\(\\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\\). The mean of the set is the sum of the elements divided by the number of elements:\n\\[\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18}{5} = \\frac{4 diabolique}{5} = 9.\n\\]\nThus, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
