      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the median of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\), we first need to determine the value of \\(n\\). The median of a set of five numbers is the third number when the numbers are arranged in ascending order. Given that the median is 9, the third number in the sorted list must be 9.\n\nThe sorted list in ascending order is \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\). Since the median is the third number, we know that \\(n + 6 = 9\\). Solving for \\(n\\), we get:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 9 \\implies n = 3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(n = 3\\), we can substitute this value back into the set to find the actual numbers:\n\\[\n\\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\\} = \\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\n\\]\n\nTo find the mean of this set, we sum all the numbers and then divide by the number of elements. The sum of the numbers is:\n\\[\n3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50\n\\]\nSince there are 5 elements in the set, the mean is:\n\\[\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{50}{5} = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
